## PEP 8 Compliance (Python)
Ensure code follows PEP 8: max 79 chars/line, snake_case for variables/functions, 
4-space indents, proper spacing around operators. Preserve functionality.

## Docstrings (Python/C++)
Add or improve docstrings for all functions, classes, and modules using Google 
Python Style Guide (Python) or Doxygen style (C++). Include parameters, returns, 
and purpose. Keep existing logic unchanged.

## Error Handling
Wrap file operations, network calls, and user inputs in try-except (Python) or 
try-catch (C++). Log errors to stderr using logging (Python) or std::cerr (C++). 
Ensure graceful failure and preserve functionality.

## Modularity
Refactor large functions into smaller, single-responsibility units. Use classes or 
modules to group related logic. Minimize global variables and ensure clear 
interfaces for extensibility.

## Code Clarity
Remove redundant code, use descriptive variable/function names, and simplify 
complex logic. Ensure code is self-explanatory for future maintainers without 
changing behavior.

## Type Safety (C++)/Type Hints (Python)
Add type hints to Python functions (PEP 484). Use explicit types in C++ (e.g., 
int32_t over int). Avoid implicit conversions to prevent bugs.

## Resource Management
Use RAII in C++ (e.g., std::unique_ptr) and context managers in Python (e.g., 
`with` for files/sockets). Ensure resources are cleaned up properly.

## Linting
Fix all pylint (Python) or clang-tidy (C++) warnings, including unused variables, 
missing imports, and style issues. Maintain existing functionality.